Chapter Two: Echoes in the Dark
The leather interior of the SUV was silent except for the low hum of the heater and the steady click of the tires navigating the winding Manali curves. In the front seat, KK kept his eyes fixed on the rearview mirror, tracking the road, while Shiv sat rigidly in the back beside Ira.

Total, suffocating silence filled the back seat. But beneath the silence, two separate storms were raging.

The physical violence of the temple had unlocked doors both of them preferred to keep bolted shut. For Ira, the suffocating grip of Bagga's men hadn't just felt like a threat in the present; it was the suffocating phantom of her past. She could practically smell the stale alcohol and hear the heavy footsteps of her father in the narrow, cramped hallways of their old Mumbai home. An only child, she had spent her entire youth dodging a violently abusive father—a prison she had barely managed to escape. But the victory had a bitter taste. Her mother was still trapped behind those locked doors in Mumbai, and every breath Ira took carried the quiet, burning vow that one day, she would earn enough, be strong enough, and save her mother from that living hell.

Beside her, Shiv was frozen in his own private nightmare. The image of Ira—a fierce, curly-haired stranger standing tall against vicious men, striking back without hesitation—had short-circuited his brain. What if my mother had been that strong? the thought echoed relentlessly in his mind.

He was just five years old when his world ended in a Mumbai slum. His drunk father had beaten his mother mercilessly before committing the ultimate, unforgivable atrocity right in front of him while little Shiv hid under the bed, paralyzed with terror. His mother’s dying words still echoed in his skull like a phantom siren: Run, son. Run. Don't ever disrespect any women... run from this monster. Here, take my bangle and keep it safe—use it in need.

In his pocket, Shiv's fingers brushed against the heavy gold bangle he had carried ever since that night. He looked sideways at Ira. She was shivering, the adrenaline finally crashing, leaving her pale and exhausted. Watching her fight had broken a wall in his chest; he didn't just admire her—he recognized a fellow survivor of the dark.

Suddenly, the weight of the night became too much. Ira's eyes fluttered closed, her breathing shallow, and before Shiv could even react, she slumped sideways, fainting entirely from physical and emotional exhaustion. Her head came to rest gently against his shoulder.

Shiv stiffened, every muscle in his body locking up from years of trauma-induced guard, but he didn't pull away. Instead, moving with slow, unnatural care, the cold and unexpressive billionaire wrapped a steady arm around her to keep her steady against the rough mountain turns, his jaw clenched as he stared out the tinted window into the dark Manali night.
